---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL } from '../lib/site.js';

// Real counts, read from the content collections, so the page cannot drift
// out of date as posts are added.
const posts = (await getCollection('blog')).filter((p) => !p.data.draft);
const postsEs = (await getCollection('blogEs')).filter((p) => !p.data.draft);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About RigFloorHQ"
  description="What RigFloorHQ is, who runs it, how we approach safety-critical material, and how to tell us when we've got something wrong."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About', href: '/about' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/about"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/about" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">About</div>
    <h1>The rig floor, <span>explained properly</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Plain-language reference for drilling, well control, welding and oilfield careers — free, independent, and honest about its limits.</p>
    <div class="page-meta">
      <span class="page-meta-item"><strong>{posts.length}</strong> articles published</span>
      <span class="page-meta-item"><strong>{postsEs.length}</strong> in Spanish</span>
      <span class="page-meta-item">No ads, no paid placement</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
      <p>
        Most oilfield knowledge lives in three places: company manuals you only see once
        you're hired, training courses that cost thousands, and the heads of people who
        have been doing it for twenty years. None of those are much use if you are trying
        to work out what a toolpusher actually does, why shut-in drill pipe pressure
        matters, or whether underwater welding is a realistic career.
      </p>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} fills that gap. We explain how the work actually functions — the
        equipment, the pressures, the procedures, the career ladder — in language that
        assumes you are capable but not that you already know. It is free to read, there is
        no signup, and there is nothing to buy.
      </p>

      <h2>Who runs it</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is an independent site run by <strong>{LEGAL.legalName}</strong>. It is
        not owned by a drilling contractor, an operator, an equipment manufacturer, a
        training provider or a recruitment agency — which matters, because each of those has
        an interest in what you conclude about training, equipment or employers.
      </p>
      <p>
        We carry no advertising, take no payment for coverage, and run no affiliate links
        or referral commissions. Nothing on this site is placed because someone paid for it
        to be there.
      </p>

      <h2>How we handle safety-critical material</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of what we write about can hurt people if it is misapplied. We take that
        seriously, and it shapes how the site is written:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Reference, never instruction.</strong> We explain how something works and
          why. We never tell you what to do on a live well. Your employer's procedures,
          the well programme, the regulations and your certified supervisor decide that —
          always, and without exception.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calculators are teaching tools.</strong> The kill sheet, hydrostatic and
          mud weight window calculators exist so you can see the arithmetic and check your
          understanding while studying. They are not approved for operational use, and we
          say so on the page rather than burying it in the terms.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Standards are named, not paraphrased into fact.</strong> Where API, IADC,
          IWCF, OSHA or similar requirements apply, we name them and point you at them.
          Standards get revised; our summary of one is a starting point, not the authority.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>We say when something varies.</strong> Practice differs between operators,
          contractors, regions and rig types. Where there is no single right answer, we say
          so instead of inventing one.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>In Spanish</h2>
      <p>
        A large part of the global drilling workforce works in Spanish. Most of the
        technical content here is published in both languages, with the
        <a href="/es">Spanish edition</a> maintained alongside the English rather than
        machine-translated and abandoned. Where a translation and the English differ, the
        English is the original.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If something here is wrong, out of date, or explained in a way that would mislead
        someone on a rig, tell us and we will fix it. On a site about safety-critical work,
        accuracy is the only thing that makes it worth publishing at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> with the page and what is
        wrong. Corrections from people who do this work for a living are especially
        welcome — if you have spotted something that reads like it was written by someone
        who has never been on a rig, we would rather hear it from you than not.
      </p>

      <h2>Using our material</h2>
      <p>
        Instructors, trainers and writers are welcome to quote us with a credit and a link
        back. If you want to use something more substantially — in a training pack, an
        induction, or a course — email and ask; the answer is usually yes.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        {LEGAL.legalName}<br />
        {LEGAL.address && <>{LEGAL.address}<br /></>}
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
      </p>

      <div class="note-box">
        <strong>One thing worth repeating.</strong> Nothing on this site replaces accredited
        training, your company's procedures, or the judgement of your supervisor. It is here
        to help you understand the work — not to authorise any part of it.
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:13px;">
        See also our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>,
        <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a> and <a href="/cookies">Cookie Policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
